---
# ansible-lint configuration. "production" profile -- the strictest one ansible-lint offers, and the
# last stop: every rule of the profile is enforced, and the families that clash with this
# repository's conventions are waived explicitly below rather than by lowering the profile.
#
# This file arrives with the first role rather than with the repository hygiene issue (#32), which
# lays down the CI: it does not decide THAT we lint, it says what "compliant" means here. The naming
# conventions it allows are the ones #31 sets, and without it the repository's first commit would be
# refused by its own linter.
#
# It asked for "safety" until #32 wired the CI up, on the reasoning that "production" was the next
# stop and could wait. Measured at that point: the tree ALREADY passed "production", so waiting
# bought nothing and cost the one moment when raising the bar is free -- a single role, no service
# block, nothing to retrofit. The rules the jump adds are the ones a second contributor forgets
# (fully-qualified module names, an explicit "changed_when" on a command, a "name" on every play
# and task), and each one is cheaper to honour from the first service than to backfill across ten.
profile: production

# Variables are named in Capitalised_Snake_Case (Artificial_Intelligence_Docker_stack_name, Docker_compose_file_name...).
# Widening the pattern keeps the rule ACTIVE: a name with a space, a dash or a leading digit is still
# an error, and that is exactly the class of typo nobody proofreads.
var_naming_pattern: "^[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*$"

skip_list:
  # Role variables are not prefixed with the role name: the prefix would be
  # "deploy_Artificial_Intelligence_Docker_stack_", longer than most of the variables it would qualify. They carry the
  # STACK's name, "Artificial_Intelligence_", which is what the caller has in mind.
  - var-naming[no-role-prefix]
  # Role names carry capitals here, the same Capitalised_Snake_Case convention as every variable
  # ("deploy_Artificial_Intelligence_Docker_stack"). Lowercasing it for this rule alone would make it
  # inconsistent with the rest of the repository rather than compliant with it.
  - role-name
  # Task names are built around the stack they act on, so the Jinja template lands mid-name:
  # "Deploy the {{ Artificial_Intelligence_Docker_stack_name }} Docker stack".
  - name[template]
